"""Chapter 3 drill items — separation of powers, the legislature, executive, judiciary."""


def q(s, o, i, sol):
    return (s, o, i, sol)


DRILL_3 = [
    q("The three organs of government are the", ["legislature, executive and judiciary", "senate, house and presidency", "federal, state and local governments", "army, police and customs"], 0, "Legislature, executive and judiciary — the three functions of making, executing and interpreting law. The others are chambers, tiers or agencies."),
    q("Separation of powers means that", ["the three organs are independent in their own sphere", "the executive controls the courts", "the legislature appoints judges", "the judiciary makes law"], 0, "Each organ exercises its own function and may not exercise another's. Control of the courts by the executive and law-making by the judiciary are precisely what the doctrine forbids."),
    q("Checks and balances are best described as", ["the division of functions among three organs", "the powers each organ has to restrain the others", "the federal division of powers", "the rules of parliamentary procedure"], 1, "The mutual brakes that keep each organ inside its sphere. The division of functions is separation of powers; the federal division is federalism."),
    q("Legislative power in Nigeria is vested in the National Assembly by", ["section 4", "section 5", "section 6", "section 9"], 0, "Section 4. Section 5 vests executive power in the President, section 6 vests judicial power in the courts, and section 9 governs amendment."),
    q("Executive power in Nigeria is vested in", ["the National Assembly", "the President", "the Supreme Court", "the Federal Executive Council"], 1, "Section 5 vests executive power in the President, exercised directly or through ministers and public officers. The Federal Executive Council is the body through which much of it is formally transacted."),
    q("Judicial power in Nigeria is vested in", ["the President", "the National Assembly", "the courts", "the Judicial Service Commission"], 2, "Section 6 vests judicial power in the courts. The Judicial Service Commission advises on appointments; it does not exercise judicial power."),
    q("The legislature is chiefly responsible for", ["executing the law", "making the law", "interpreting the law", "prosecuting offenders"], 1, "Making law. Executing is the executive, interpreting is the judiciary, and prosecution belongs to the executive's law officers."),
    q("Which of the following is a function of the legislature?", ["Approving the annual budget", "Commanding the armed forces", "Deciding election petitions", "Issuing currency"], 0, "Appropriation is a legislative function: no money leaves the Consolidated Revenue Fund without it. Command of the forces is executive, election petitions are judicial, and currency is the central bank's."),
    q("The Nigerian National Assembly consists of", ["a Senate of 109 and a House of Representatives of 360", "a Senate of 100 and a House of 360", "a single chamber of 469 members", "a Senate of 36 and a House of 774"], 0, "109 senators — three per state plus one for the FCT — and 360 representatives. Together they make 469, but as two chambers, not one."),
    q("A bicameral legislature is one with", ["one chamber", "two chambers", "three chambers", "no chamber"], 1, "Two chambers. Nigeria's is the Senate and the House of Representatives, and the merits claimed for it are second thoughts and better representation of the units."),
    q("Which of the following is an advantage of a bicameral legislature?", ["It is cheaper than a single chamber", "It allows a second look at legislation", "It makes law-making faster", "It removes the need for committees"], 1, "A second chamber gives second thoughts and represents the states as units. It is more expensive, slower and adds duplication — the standard demerits."),
    q("The President's refusal to sign a bill is called", ["an amendment", "a veto", "a filibuster", "a resolution"], 1, "A veto. The National Assembly may override it with a two-thirds majority of each House, which is the check on the check."),
    q("The power of the National Assembly to remove the President for gross misconduct is called", ["impeachment", "recall", "veto", "censure"], 0, "Impeachment, followed by removal on a two-thirds vote of the Senate. Recall is the removal of a legislator by the electorate, and censure is a formal disapproval without removal."),
    q("Which of the following is a function of the executive?", ["Making laws", "Executing laws and formulating policy", "Interpreting the constitution", "Adjudicating election petitions"], 1, "The executive executes the law and initiates policy. Law-making is legislative, and interpretation and election petitions are judicial."),
    q("In a parliamentary system, the executive is", ["elected separately from the legislature", "drawn from and answerable to the legislature", "appointed by the judiciary", "chosen by the army"], 1, "Ministers are members of the legislature, and the government survives only while it holds a majority there. A separately elected executive is the presidential model."),
    q("In a presidential system of government", ["the President is a member of the legislature", "the executive and legislature are separately elected", "the President is chosen by the legislature", "the cabinet is drawn from parliament"], 1, "Separate election is the defining feature. The President is not a member of the legislature, and ministers are appointed from outside it."),
    q("The head of government in a parliamentary system is the", ["monarch", "Prime Minister", "Speaker", "Chief Justice"], 1, "The Prime Minister. The monarch or ceremonial president is head of state, the Speaker presides over the lower House, and the Chief Justice heads the judiciary."),
    q("Which of the following is a function of the judiciary?", ["Making law", "Interpreting the law and adjudicating disputes", "Formulating policy", "Approving the budget"], 1, "Interpretation and adjudication. Making law is legislative, and policy and the budget belong to the executive and legislature respectively."),
    q("The power of a court to declare an Act unconstitutional is called", ["judicial precedent", "judicial review", "delegated legislation", "the covering-the-field doctrine"], 1, "Judicial review tests validity against the Constitution. Precedent is the binding force of past decisions, delegated legislation is made by a subordinate body, and covering the field decides conflicts between federal and state laws."),
    q("An appeal differs from a judicial review because an appeal", ["tests the validity of a statute", "asks a higher court whether a decision was right", "is heard only by a magistrate", "is decided by the legislature"], 1, "An appeal reviews a decision on the facts or the law; a review tests whether an Act or an executive act is valid at all."),
    q("The highest court in Nigeria is the", ["Court of Appeal", "Federal High Court", "Supreme Court", "National Industrial Court"], 2, "The Supreme Court, established by section 230. The Court of Appeal sits below it, and the Federal High Court and the National Industrial Court are courts of first instance in their own fields."),
    q("The number of justices of the Supreme Court of Nigeria is", ["seven", "twelve", "twenty-one, plus the Chief Justice", "fifteen"], 2, "The Supreme Court consists of the Chief Justice and twenty-one other justices — a number fixed by the Constitution since 2011. Seven is the quorum for a sitting, which is the option written to catch you."),
    q("Judicial independence in Nigeria is protected by", ["security of tenure and salaries charged to the Consolidated Revenue Fund", "the President's power to dismiss judges", "the control of the courts by the Ministry of Justice", "the appointment of judges by the Senate alone"], 0, "Tenure and money: a judge may be removed only for inability or misconduct on a Senate address, and salaries are charged to the Consolidated Revenue Fund so they cannot be withheld."),
    q("A judge of the Supreme Court of Nigeria may be removed by", ["the President alone", "the Chief Justice", "the President on an address supported by two-thirds of the Senate", "a simple majority of the National Assembly"], 2, "Removal follows an address by the President supported by two-thirds of the Senate, on grounds of inability or misconduct — the mechanism that makes tenure secure."),
    q("Which of the following courts is at the base of the Nigerian judicial hierarchy?", ["The Supreme Court", "The Court of Appeal", "The magistrate and district courts", "The Federal High Court"], 2, "The magistrate, district, customary and Sharia courts, whose decisions may be appealed upward. The Supreme Court is at the top, not the base."),
    q("The judicial body that advises on the appointment of judges in Nigeria is the", ["Federal Judicial Service Commission", "National Assembly", "Federal Executive Council", "Nigerian Bar Association"], 0, "The Federal Judicial Service Commission advises the President on appointments to the federal courts; the National Judicial Council recommends and disciplinarily controls judges. The Bar Association is a professional body."),
    q("Fundamental human rights in Nigeria may be enforced by an application to", ["the National Assembly", "a High Court", "the President", "the police"], 1, "Section 46 allows a person whose Chapter IV right is breached to apply to a High Court for redress. The legislature legislates, the President and the police are the potential violators."),
    q("Delegated legislation is law made by", ["the National Assembly", "the courts", "a body to which the legislature has granted the power", "the electorate at a referendum"], 2, "A minister, council, corporation or agency acting under powers granted by an Act. Law made by the courts is precedent, and a referendum is direct legislation by the people."),
    q("Which of the following is a reason for delegated legislation?", ["It removes the need for a legislature", "It saves parliamentary time and uses technical expertise", "It makes the courts unnecessary", "It allows ministers to amend the constitution"], 1, "Time, expertise, flexibility and emergency are the standard reasons. Delegated legislation does not replace the legislature, the courts or the amending procedure."),
    q("A regulation made by a minister beyond the powers granted by the enabling Act is said to be", ["ultra vires", "intra vires", "a precedent", "a constitutional amendment"], 0, "Ultra vires — beyond the powers. The courts will declare such a regulation void, which is the main judicial control on delegated legislation."),
    q("Which of the following is a control on delegated legislation?", ["Publication in the gazette", "The army's approval", "A referendum on every regulation", "The Speaker's signature"], 0, "Publication in the gazette, scrutiny by legislative committees, the power of annulment in the enabling Act, and judicial review are the controls. Armies and referendums have no part in it."),
    q("The civil service is best described as", ["the elected officials of the executive", "the permanent body of officials who administer government departments", "the personal staff of the President", "the judges and magistrates"], 1, "Permanent officials who administer departments and advise ministers. Elected officials and personal staff come and go with a government; the judiciary is a separate organ."),
    q("The civil service is said to be anonymous because", ["its officials are unknown to the public", "its officials do not publicly defend policy, which is the minister's responsibility", "its records are secret from the courts", "its officials cannot be disciplined"], 1, "Anonymity is about public responsibility: the minister answers for policy, and the official is not named in the debate. It does not mean that officials are unknown or unaccountable."),
    q("The doctrine of the rule of law requires that", ["public officials are above the law", "the law applies equally to the government and the governed", "the executive may override the courts", "judges make policy"], 1, "Equality before the law is the second limb of Dicey's rule of law. Officials above the law, executive override of courts and judicial policy-making are its opposites."),
    q("Which of the following best illustrates a check by the legislature on the executive?", ["The President vetoing a bill", "The Senate confirming a ministerial nominee", "A court striking down a decree", "INEC conducting an election"], 1, "Confirmation of appointments is a legislative brake on executive appointments. A veto runs the other way, a court's decision is judicial, and an election is the electorate's check."),
    q("The President's power to grant a pardon is an example of", ["a check on the judiciary", "a legislative function", "a delegated power of the courts", "a prerogative of mercy exercised on the advice of the Council of State"], 3, "The prerogative of mercy, exercised on the Council of State's advice. It tempers the judicial outcome but is not a reversal of the conviction."),
    q("A bill becomes law in Nigeria when", ["it is introduced in the Senate", "it has passed both Houses and received the President's assent, or the veto is overridden", "it is debated at the second reading", "the Speaker signs it"], 1, "Passage through both chambers plus assent, or an override of the veto by two-thirds of each House. Introduction, debate and signature by a presiding officer are stages, not the completion."),
    q("Which of the following is NOT a function of the legislature?", ["Representing the electorate", "Overseeing the executive", "Adjudicating criminal cases", "Appropriating public funds"], 2, "Adjudication is judicial. Representation, oversight and appropriation are three of the legislature's principal functions."),
    q("The independence of the judiciary is threatened when", ["judges' salaries are charged to the Consolidated Revenue Fund", "the executive ignores court orders", "judges have security of tenure", "appointments are made on the Judicial Service Commission's advice"], 1, "Ignoring a court order is the practical death of independence; the other three options are the guarantees of it."),
    q("The Chief Justice of Nigeria is appointed by", ["the Senate alone", "the President on the recommendation of the National Judicial Council, subject to Senate confirmation", "the Judicial Service Commission alone", "the House of Representatives"], 1, "The President appoints on the Council's recommendation and the Senate confirms — a procedure that involves the executive and the legislature while leaving the choice to the judges."),
]
